 "result_text": "I fixed the column widths. Columns `h` and `mu` now size to their longest cell, so `0.0` and `0.0` no longer overflow. I didn't run your exact example or the repo's test suite. I checked with a small two-column table that has a `0.0` row and a `None` row. The widths came out right there.\n\nThe cause was in `find_widths` in `src/pyRestTable/rest_table.py`. It measured each cell with `str(_ or '')`, which turns falsy values like `0` and `0.0` into an empty string. The width calculation therefore counted them as zero characters, but the rows still printed them as `0.0`.\n\nI changed line 380 to measure `str(_)`, so the width matches what actually gets printed. One side effect: a `None` cell now counts as the 4 characters of `None`, because that's how it prints. Before, it counted as empty.\n\nI didn't touch the tests or commit anything.",
